PuTTY supports the meta key by prefixing ESC to things. Most
applications seem happy with this behaviour; NetHack is the only one
I know of which isn't happy with anything short of setting the high
bit.

The trouble with setting the high bit is that it only really makes
sense in the limited circumstance of pressing Meta plus a
single-byte character (i.e. not a function key), on a terminal using
a single-byte character set. What should happen if the user presses
Meta-F1, or Meta-a in a UTF-8 terminal, is a question for a wiser
head than mine. We could support it as a non-default option and deal
with all difficult cases by refusing to generate any input at all,
or falling back to the normal ESC behaviour, but this doesn't seem
likely to be what people really want.

If anyone can justify a clear specification of how high-bit-meta
should work in PuTTY, which covers all cases including function
keys, UTF-8 and both at once, I'll be happy to consider it. I've
tried and failed :-/

(I'm also inclined to wonder whether NH should be `fixed' in this
regard; as far as I'm aware sequences like ESC-c aren't currently
used for anything else. This would be conceptually much simpler than
the corresponding fix on the PuTTY side...)
